# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Luke 7:36 reads, “And one of the Pharisees desired him that he would eat with him.” Jesus went into the Pharisee’s house and “sat down to meat.” Luke names the Pharisee in 7:40, where Jesus calls him Simon. Luke does not say in 7:36 what the occasion was or who else was invited. Verse 49 shows that others “sat at meat with him.”'))
A(('p', '2.2 The woman. “And, behold, a woman in the city, which was a sinner, when she knew that Jesus sat at meat in the Pharisee’s house, brought an alabaster box of ointment” (7:37). She “stood at his feet behind him weeping, and began to wash his feet with tears, and did wipe them with the hairs of her head, and kissed his feet, and anointed them with the ointment” (7:38). Luke says she is “a sinner” and does not say what her sin was. He says she knew where Jesus sat at meat, and he does not say that she had met Him before or why she came. She says nothing in the scene.'))
A(('p', '2.3 What Simon thought. “Now when the Pharisee which had bidden him saw it, he spake within himself, saying, This man, if he were a prophet, would have known who and what manner of woman this is that toucheth him: for she is a sinner” (7:39). Simon does not speak aloud. Luke then writes, “And Jesus answering said unto him, Simon, I have somewhat to say unto thee. And he saith, Master, say on” (7:40). Luke does not say how Jesus knew what Simon had said within himself, and nothing is added here to explain it.'))
A(('call', 'The Trigger Is a Thought',
   'No question is put to Jesus aloud. Simon “spake within himself” (Luke 7:39), and Luke says that Jesus, answering, said, “Simon, I have somewhat to say unto thee” (7:40). The story of the two debtors begins in the next verse, and it is told to Simon.'))
A(('p', '2.4 What stands before the dinner in Luke 7. The chapter has John’s disciples and Jesus’ words about John (7:18–28). Luke then writes that “all the people that heard him, and the publicans, justified God” (7:29), and that “the Pharisees and lawyers rejected the counsel of God against themselves” (7:30). Jesus says of Himself that men say, “a friend of publicans and sinners” (7:34). Luke does not say that the dinner of 7:36 follows on the same day, and nothing here makes the earlier verses an explanation of the story.'))
A(('p', '2.5 The prophetic context. Jesus says of His ministry, “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (Luke 4:43). Luke 8:1 says that He went throughout every city and village “preaching and shewing the glad tidings of the kingdom of God.” Luke 7:36–50 does not use the word kingdom, and the story is read as told within that ministry and not as a saying that floats free of it.'))
A(('p', '2.6 Why Jesus tells it here. Luke gives the occasion himself: Simon’s unspoken thought about the woman and about Jesus (7:39), and Jesus’ words, “I have somewhat to say unto thee” (7:40). The story is not said to be told for any other reason.'))
