A(('p', '4.2 Jesus’ application (7:47). “Wherefore I say unto thee, Her sins, which are many, are forgiven; for she loved much: but to whom little is forgiven, the same loveth little.” These are Jesus’ own words, and they are the only place in the passage where Jesus draws a conclusion from the story. The words are given as He spoke them. The text joins two statements about the woman with the word “for,” and then sets “but to whom little is forgiven, the same loveth little” beside them. The study records the words and does not add a doctrine of how forgiveness is obtained.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of what Jesus says', 'What Jesus says', 'Verse'],
    ['How He introduces it', '“Wherefore I say unto thee”', 'Luke 7:47'],
    ['About the woman’s sins', '“Her sins, which are many, are forgiven”', 'Luke 7:47'],
    ['About the woman’s love', '“for she loved much”', 'Luke 7:47'],
    ['About the one forgiven little', '“but to whom little is forgiven, the same loveth little”', 'Luke 7:47'],
], [0.28, 0.54, 0.18]))
A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus says to the woman, and what the guests say. “And he said unto her, Thy sins are forgiven” (7:48). “And they that sat at meat with him began to say within themselves, Who is this that forgiveth sins also?” (7:49). “And he said to the woman, Thy faith hath saved thee; go in peace” (7:50). Verse 49 is the saying of the guests, “within themselves,” and not the words of Jesus. Verses 48 and 50 are Jesus’ words to the woman in that house. They are recorded here as written, and the sentence in 7:50 is not carried to any other person or setting.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What Jesus does not name in the story. Jesus does not identify the creditor. Jesus does not identify the debtor who owed five hundred pence. Jesus does not identify the debtor who owed fifty. Jesus does not identify the five hundred pence or the fifty. Jesus does not identify the forgiving of the debts. Jesus does not say that the two debtors are Simon and the woman. In 7:47 He says, “Her sins, which are many, are forgiven,” and “to whom little is forgiven,” and He does not say in so many words that the second phrase is Simon.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus names what He does in Luke 7:44–47 and nothing more, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the creditor is a certain person, that the five hundred pence is a certain kind of sin, that the debtors are certain persons, or that the woman’s love is the price of her forgiveness is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.5 How the words stand in sequence. In the story the forgiving is told in 7:42 and the question about love follows it. In 7:47 Jesus says “Her sins, which are many, are forgiven; for she loved much.” The words of the story and the words of the application are each reported in the order Luke gives them. The study does not decide from either order how forgiveness and love are related, and it does not say that the woman’s acts of 7:38 were the ground of her forgiveness.'))
A(('p', '4.6 What the words stay with. The words of 7:44–50 were spoken in a Pharisee’s house to Simon, to the woman and in the hearing of the guests. They belong to that scene, and they do not supply identifications that the story leaves unnamed.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It repeats none of Jesus’ own words from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Jesus tells Simon a story with two debtors who could not pay and a creditor who forgave both. He then asks a question: which of them will love him most? The meaning of the comparison lies in the movement of the story: a debt, an inability to pay, a free forgiving, and a question about the love that follows.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A debt that cannot be paid is forgiven freely.** The debtors “had nothing to pay,” and the creditor “frankly forgave them both.” The story does not ask for payment and does not set a condition.',
    '**The debts differ in size.** One debt is ten times the other, and the story forgives both. The comparison puts a larger forgiving beside a smaller one.',
    '**The question ties love to what was forgiven.** Jesus asks which debtor will love most, and Simon’s answer, “he, to whom he forgave most,” is the answer Jesus calls rightly judged.',
    '**The hearer’s own judgment is the measure.** Simon answers the question himself, and Jesus tells him that he has judged rightly. The comparison leads the hearer to give the answer.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. Simon had said within himself that a prophet would have known the kind of woman who touched Him (7:39). He was meant to understand that the comparison speaks to his own unspoken thought: that forgiveness and love are measured in a way he had agreed to with his own answer. The guests who sat at meat were in the room, and the woman was there. Luke does not say that the story was told to any of them but Simon.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The story sets a forgiven debt beside the love that answers it. It shows that the one who is forgiven more has more reason to love. It does not say that love is the payment for the forgiving. The creditor forgave “when they had nothing to pay,” and the story does not report that either debtor paid him back with love before the forgiving.'))
A(('p', '5.5 The Kingdom setting. The story is told within the ministry in which Jesus said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43; 8:1). The meaning of the comparison is read as part of that ministry to the people of Israel. It is not read as a statement about the church of the Body.'))
A(('p', '5.6 What this section does not do. It does not say who the creditor is, who the debtors are or what the pence stand for. It does not say that Simon is the one who owed fifty or that the woman is the one who owed five hundred. It does not turn forgiving, loving or believing into steps by which a person is saved. It draws no meaning from any other parable into this story.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The Two Debtors is told in Luke 7. It is kept apart here from three neighbors and from the other anointing accounts.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 18:21–35', '“Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him?”', 'The Unforgiving Servant. A king, a servant and a fellow servant, with a large debt and a small one. It has its own setting and its own study. Its debts are not these debts.'],
    ['Luke 18:9–14', '“certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous”', 'The Pharisee and the Tax Collector. Two men go up to the temple to pray. Its Pharisee is not Simon, and its setting is not a dinner.'],
    ['Luke 10:25–37', '“a certain lawyer stood up, and tempted him”', 'The Good Samaritan. Told after a lawyer’s question, with a traveler on a road. It has its own study and no creditor or debtors.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The Two Debtors is told in a Pharisee’s house, after Simon’s unspoken thought about a woman who was a sinner (7:36–39). The Unforgiving Servant is told in answer to Peter’s question about forgiving a brother (Matthew 18:21). The Pharisee and the Tax Collector is told “unto certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others” (Luke 18:9). The Good Samaritan is told after a lawyer’s question about eternal life (10:25). Each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The Two Debtors has a creditor and two debtors, a Pharisee named Simon and a woman who was a sinner. The Unforgiving Servant has a king, a servant and a fellow servant. The Pharisee and the Tax Collector has a Pharisee and a publican. The Good Samaritan has a lawyer, a traveler, a priest, a Levite and a Samaritan. No character is carried from one account to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The Two Debtors moves from a debt that cannot be paid to a forgiving of both debts and a question about love. The Unforgiving Servant moves from a forgiven debt to a servant who will not forgive, and the forgiving is later undone. In the Two Debtors the story reports no undoing. The Pharisee and the Tax Collector moves from two prayers to a closing saying. The Good Samaritan moves from a question to a road and to a question again. None of those movements is read into this story.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the sums in the Two Debtors, “five hundred pence” and “fifty,” are not the sums of the Unforgiving Servant, and Jesus does not join them. Second, Luke 7 has a Pharisee named Simon, and this Simon is not said to be the Pharisee of Luke 18. Third, the woman of Luke 7:37–38 is not joined here to the anointings of Matthew 26:6–13, Mark 14:3–9 and John 12:1–8. Matthew 26:6 has “Simon the leper” and John 12 has another setting. Each account is read in its own place, and Jesus does not say that they are one.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks in a Pharisee’s house to Simon, in the presence of the woman and the guests (Luke 7:36–50). The story is told within the earthly ministry in which He said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43) and went throughout every city and village preaching the glad tidings of the kingdom of God (8:1).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The Two Debtors was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Luke 7:36–50 speaks of the Body of Christ, and the passage is not read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The forgiving of the debtors, the forgiving of the woman’s sins, the love she shows and the faith that Jesus speaks of are not a description of the Body of Christ. The story is not read as a universal picture of how any person is forgiven. What Jesus says in 7:47, 48 and 50 is spoken to Simon and to the woman in His earthly ministry, and it is not carried over as a requirement for the Body. Forgiving, loving and believing are an act in a story and words in a scene, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this scene: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of honest accounting.** The story sets a large debt and a small one side by side. A reader learns to weigh what he owes honestly and not to count it small because someone else’s looks large.',
    '**A lesson of gratitude in proportion.** The creditor forgave both, and the question asks which will love most. A reader learns that gratitude grows with a clear sense of how much has been forgiven.',
    '**A lesson of attention.** Jesus sets what Simon did not give beside what the woman did (7:44–46). A reader learns to see the person in front of him before judging.',
    '**A lesson of weighing a private judgment.** Simon spoke only within himself, and Jesus answered it. A reader learns to test his unspoken judgments of others.',
    '**A lesson of not hurrying to condemn.** Simon said that she is a sinner. A reader learns that a person may be known by what he sees of her and still be misjudged.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the debtor, that the Body is the woman, or that the Body forgives as the creditor does. They are principles drawn from what the story and the scene show.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus tells Simon of a certain creditor who had two debtors, one owing five hundred pence and the other fifty. They had nothing to pay, and he frankly forgave them both. He asks which will love him most, and when Simon answers “he, to whom he forgave most,” Jesus says he has rightly judged. Jesus then sets what Simon did not give beside what the woman did, says her many sins are forgiven for she loved much, and says the one to whom little is forgiven loveth little. He tells the woman her sins are forgiven and her faith has saved her. The passage does not say who the creditor or the debtors are, what the debts were, or how forgiveness and love are related beyond the words Jesus speaks. Any reading that adds a creditor, a debtor or a way of being forgiven goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a certain creditor, two debtors, five hundred pence and fifty, nothing to pay, a free forgiving of both, a question about love, Simon’s answer and Jesus’ verdict.',
    '**The meaning:** a debt that cannot be paid is forgiven freely, and the love that follows is measured by what was forgiven.',
    '**The boundaries:** only what Jesus says in 7:44–47 is named; the creditor, the debtors and the pence are not.',
    '**The present lesson:** honest accounting, proportionate gratitude, attention, weighing a private judgment, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

